// `std:fmt` — pure-VL number formatting AND number parsing over the intrinsic
// floor (docs/internals/std-design.md D4 item 1). The exported helpers take a
// `self` first parameter, so they read as UFCS methods on the receiver
// (`n.toString()`, `"1.5".parseF64()`, `parts.join(sep)`).
//
// THE EXPORT WAS CALLED `toStr` UNTIL 2026-09-01, AND THE COMPILER CARRIED AN
// AMBIENT BUILTIN CALLED `toString`. Owner ruling, executed in that change: the
// builtin is retired and this export takes its name. `toString` is the
// cross-language-conventional spelling (Java, C#, JS, Kotlin, Scala, Dart all
// spell it in full; Rust's `to_string` is the same word), and after the ruling
// there is exactly ONE of it in the language, at the std surface, reached by an
// ordinary import. DECISIONS.md carries the ruling; what follows is the
// measurement trail that settled it, kept because a reader asking "why is this
// not a builtin?" is asking about these three facts.
//
// (1) NO CAPABILITY WAS LOST. The builtin's domain was `i32 | boolean` — a
// STRICT SUBSET of this export's `i32 | i64 | boolean | f64`; measured
// 2026-09-01 on the pre-rename compiler, `toString(n)` over an i64 and
// `toString(1.5)` both refused. Every program the builtin served, this export
// serves, and two widths more.
//
// (2) THE RENAME BOUGHT THE UFCS SPELLING, which is the actual user-facing win.
// Builtins are not `self`-first, so `n.toString()` refused on the pre-rename
// compiler (`member access '.toString' on non-object i32`) while `n.toStr()`
// worked. After it, `n.toString()` is the method spelling and `toString(n)` the
// free-function one, both reaching the same function.
//
// (3) THE ONE BREAK, shipped deliberately and once: `toString(42)` compiled with
// NO import before and needs `import { toString } from "std:fmt"` now. std has
// no deprecation story, so the compiler pays for the break with a targeted
// refusal instead — `typecheck.stdFmtMovedNote` appends the import line to the
// undeclared-identifier message AND to the member-access-on-a-scalar sentence,
// for BOTH `toString` and the old `toStr` spelling (four assertions in
// `tests/cases/tostring/moved-to-std-hint.vl`; the `toStr` note carries an
// expiry, `toString`'s does not), following the `then`-removal precedent
// (#2094).
//
// WHAT IS WRITTEN HERE is the number rendering — `toString` over the two integer
// widths, boolean, and `f64` — plus the three inverses that read a number back:
// `parseI64`, `parseI32` and `parseF64`. Each half has its own design block
// below and each block is the thing to read before touching its functions:
// `TEXT → INTEGER` owns the two integer parsers (their grammar is deliberately
// a strict SUBSET of the float one, so picking between them is a choice of type
// and never of dialect, and out of range is `null` in the integer block where
// it is ±Infinity in the float one), and `f64 ↔ TEXT` owns the
// interesting halves — the shortest-round-trip renderer and its correctly
// rounded reader.
//
// THE NAME IS BORROWED AND THE DOMAIN IS NOT. In the six languages cited above
// `toString` is defined on every value; here it is exactly `i32 | i64 |
// boolean | f64`. Measured 2026-09-01: `"abc".toString()` → `no method
// '.toString' on string`, `[1,2].toString()` → `… on array i32[]`,
// `p.toString()` on a struct → `no field 'toString' on Point`. A string
// identity arm is deliberately absent — `s.toString()` is `s`, and an arm that
// does nothing invites the name to grow into a universal renderer VL has no
// overloading or traits to deliver (the derived `show<T>` is that future,
// serde stage 2). And because the namespace is flat, a file that declares its
// own `toString` cannot also import this one: that is a HARD refusal
// (`Duplicate binding "toString"` —
// `tests/cases/tostring/import-and-declare-collide.vl`), and
// `import { toString as … }` is the escape.
//
// WHAT IS NOT, ON THE NUMERIC SIDE: `f32`, in either direction, and any radix
// but ten. Neither is "later here"; both are a different computation.
//
//   `f32` HAS NO ARM AND SHOULD NOT GET A WRAPPER. An f32's shortest rendering
//   is shorter than its widened f64's — `0.1` against `0.10000000149011612` —
//   so it is Burger–Dybvig with 24-bit-significand boundaries, not `toString` of
//   the widened value, and reading one back needs f32-nearest rounding rather
//   than f64-nearest then a narrowing. `docs/serde-design.md` stage 0 carries
//   it as the remaining half.
//
//   THAT ABSENCE HAS A DIAGNOSTIC PRICE AND IT IS THE F64 ARM'S FAULT, so it
//   is stated rather than left to be hit. An `f32` widens losslessly into the
//   `f64` arm, so the checker now ACCEPTS `x.toString()` over an `f32` binding
//   where it used to refuse it cleanly (`member access '.toString' on non-object
//   f32`); the emitter then floors with `emitProgram: union atom has no value
//   box`. Measured 2026-09-01, both spellings. The hole is the COMPILER's and
//   predates this module — three std-free lines reproduce it, and it refuses
//   identically on a master-built seed — but it was unreachable from `toString`
//   until the f64 arm existed. Pinned by
//   `scripts/capability-probes/f32-into-f64-union-arm.vl`, whose header argues
//   the end state is to BOX the widened value rather than to re-narrow the
//   checker: the widening is lossless, so clause 2 says the program must
//   build. When it does, an `f32` will render as the f64 it became, which is
//   why an f32 arm is still owed after that.
//
//   RADIX: `toString` is decimal only. Hex, binary and octal renderings are a
//   different function family with their own case and padding questions, and
//   nothing in the tree asks for one.
//
// WHAT IS NOT is the string manipulation. `split`, `join`, `repeat` and
// `padLeft` used to have their implementations in this file; they now live in
// `std:str`, which owns the whole string method surface
// (`docs/guide/strings-design.md` §Methods, step 1), and the names below are a
// re-export plus one delegating call. Nothing an existing importer spells has
// changed — `"5".padLeft(4, "ab")` is still `"aba5"` — and `join` got FASTER on
// the way: `std:str` fills one code-point buffer where the version here ran an
// `out = out + parts[i]` accumulator, which is O(n²) on this compiler (the B7b
// fusion that used to hide that lives only in the deleted TS emitter — the
// measurement is in `std:str`'s header).
//
// THE MOVE WAS NOT TIDINESS. VL has no namespace import, so every export lands
// in the importer's flat top-level scope and a name declared twice across two
// imported modules is a HARD PARSE ERROR. Two modules each carrying their own
// `split` would mean a file could import `toString` and `split` from `std:fmt`, or
// `trim` and `split` from `std:str`, but never `toString` and `trim` and `split` —
// and whichever module the caller reached for would have silently decided
// whether their `join` was O(n) or O(n²). One implementation, one home, and
// this module keeps the names its importers already spell.
//
// WHAT THE MOVE COSTS, MEASURED — AND THE NUMBER MOVES WHENEVER THIS FILE
// GROWS, so it is dated. There is no cross-module dead-code elimination, so a
// program that imports ONLY `toString` emits `std:str` too AND every byte of the
// f64 halves below. `import { toString } from "std:fmt"` + one `n.toString()` on an
// i32, built to wasm on 2026-09-01: **17,645 bytes** — 2,850 before the
// `std:str` move, 7,548 before the f64 half, 17,062 before the integer parsers,
// and the breakdown of the 9,514 the f64 half added is in the `f64 ↔ TEXT`
// header, where it is the argument for `parseF64` living here. The integer
// parsers' own **583** is an A/B on ONE seed (this file against `HEAD`'s, same
// binary, `VL_STD` pinned to each) rather than a subtraction across two
// measurements — and it was re-run after the leading-"+" arm came out, which
// moved it by 19 bytes from the 602 an earlier draft measured. That is a real
// regression and it is recorded here rather than
// left to be discovered, but it is a symptom of the missing DCE and not of the
// module shape — the same program already carried this module's own unused
// `split`/`join`/`repeat` before the move, for the same reason. The fix is
// whole-program dead-code elimination, which nothing in the pipeline does
// today; the alternative — two implementations of `split`, so that a
// formatting import stays small — buys the bytes back by paying in the hazard
// above.
//
// AND THAT NUMBER IS THE `none` RUNG — AND THE PROBE IS CONSTANT-FOLDABLE. The
// same program is 1,041 bytes at `-O` and 542 at `-O3`, but at `-O3` the module
// holds exactly ONE function (`wasm-dis p.wasm | grep -c '^ (func'` → 1):
// binaryen evaluated `toString(42)` at compile time, so 542 prices the FOLD,
// not the renderer. With the value not compile-time known (a loop-accumulated
// one), `-O3` is 650 for the i32 arm, 5,164 once the f64 arm is live, and
// 7,806 with `parseF64` live too (the i32 rung re-measured 2026-09-01 with the
// header's own recipe and this file's own A/B; it read 652 before a `fmt.vl`
// edit, and two numbers for one rung in one paragraph is unreadable, so the
// stale one is gone rather than annotated). The integer parsers add NOTHING to
// that rung when nothing calls them — 650 bytes on both arms of the same A/B,
// so binaryen drops them entirely, and their 583 is a `none`-rung number only.
// DCE does find what
// the VL pipeline does not — but quote the rung AND what the program reaches:
// the standing cost is ~0.6 KB for integer rendering and ~5 KB once a float is
// rendered, and the same correction applies to every "importing `std:fmt`
// costs …" claim in the tree (`std:utf8`'s header carries all three rungs).
//
// `padLeft` IS THE ONE NAME THAT DID NOT MOVE VERBATIM. `std:str` spells it
// `padStart`, matching `padEnd` beside it and the JS pair both are modelled on;
// `padLeft`/`padRight` is the other convention and picking one mattered less
// than not shipping both. The wrapper below keeps this module's spelling for
// its existing callers at the cost of one call, and it is a DELEGATION rather
// than a copy — there is still exactly one padder in the tree.

import { padStart } from "std:str"

// The string surface, re-exported from its owner. A re-export records a NAME,
// so these are the SAME functions `std:str` exports and not second copies —
// importing `split` from either module reaches one implementation. (The same
// mechanism `std:args` uses to republish `Utf8Error`.)
export { join, repeat, split } from "std:str"

// Decimal digit d in 0..=9 as its character. Takes i64 so both integer
// widths share one renderer (i64 cannot narrow to i32, so a string-index
// lookup is not expressible for the wide path).
function digitChar(d: i64) {
  if d == 0 { return "0" }
  if d == 1 { return "1" }
  if d == 2 { return "2" }
  if d == 3 { return "3" }
  if d == 4 { return "4" }
  if d == 5 { return "5" }
  if d == 6 { return "6" }
  if d == 7 { return "7" }
  if d == 8 { return "8" }
  "9"
}

// Decimal rendering of any i64, including i64 min. The accumulator works in
// the NEGATIVE range (positive inputs are negated first) because the negative
// range covers i64 min, whose magnitude overflows when negated; `%` on a
// negative operand yields a digit in -9..=0, negated back per digit.
function renderI64(n: i64) {
  if n == 0 { return "0" }
  const zero: i64 = 0
  let m = n
  if m > 0 { m = zero - m }
  let out = ""
  while m < 0 {
    out = digitChar(zero - (m % 10)) + out
    m = m / 10
  }
  if n < 0 { out = "-" + out }
  out
}

// Decimal rendering of a number via its receiver: `n.toString()` over an i32, an
// i64 or an f64 binding. One union receiver covers every width (UFCS resolves
// by `self`+arity; the value boxes in, `is`-narrows out); i32 widens
// losslessly into the shared i64 renderer. The boolean arm renders true/false.
//
// The f64 arm is the whole `f64 ↔ TEXT` section below, and it is a SEPARATE
// arm of this same union rather than a separate exported name because a flat
// namespace has room for exactly one `toString` and a reader who has learned
// `n.toString()` should not have to learn a second spelling to render a float.
// Measured, so it is not an assumption: a four-arm receiver `is`-narrows and
// monomorphizes per arm on this compiler — `f64bits` inside the `is f64`
// branch reads the narrowed f64, no boxing artefact survives.
export function toString(self: i32 | i64 | boolean | f64) {
  if self is boolean {
    if self { return "true" }
    return "false"
  }
  if self is i32 {
    const wide: i64 = self
    return renderI64(wide)
  }
  if self is f64 { return renderF64(self) }
  return renderI64(self)
}

// `padLeft` is this module's historical spelling of `std:str`'s `padStart` and
// is kept for its existing importers. Pads `self` on the left with `fill` up to
// `width`: a multi-character fill is tiled and truncated so the result is
// exactly `width` long, and `self` comes back unchanged when it is already wide
// enough or when `fill` is empty. New code should reach for `padStart` (and its
// `padEnd` twin, which has no name here).
export function padLeft(self: string, width: i32, fill: string) {
  return padStart(self, width, fill)
}

// ── TEXT → INTEGER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `parseI64` and `parseI32` are the EXACT inverses of `toString` over the two
// integer widths, and "exact" is the whole warrant. Until they landed the only
// text→number path in the tree was the `parseF64` funnel, which cannot be exact
// wherever the integer is not representable as a double: measured 2026-09-01,
// `parseF64("9007199254740993")` is `9007199254740992`, and
// `parseF64("9223372036854775807")` is 2^63 — it PRINTS as
// `9223372036854776000`, which is the shortest decimal that round-trips and is
// not itself a double — while `parseI64` of that string is the integer itself.
//
// SERDE DECISION B (RULED 2026-09-01: `i64` on the JSON wire is ALWAYS a
// NUMBER — `docs/internals/serde-critique-synthesis.md` §"Decisions that are
// the owner's", `docs/serde-design.md` OQ-9) is NOT what admits these two
// exports, and this module does not depend on it. What admits them is the
// sentence in that same recommendation: they are needed "regardless … by every
// option, and today the ONLY number path is the funnel". A decimal-string wire
// would need an exact integer READER even more than a numeric one does.
//
// `std-design.md` D2's inventory clause admits it on the same
// line that admits `std:fmt` itself — "what the LANGUAGE story needs to be
// complete without third parties" — and the consumer that will spell it is
// `std:json`, next in the same slice.
//
// ── THE GRAMMAR, a strict SUBSET of `parseF64`'s, deliberately:
//
//     "-"? digit+
//
// and nothing else — no whitespace at either end, no digit separator, no
// `0x`/`0b`, no radix point, no exponent, no leading "+". So `"1.0"` and
// `"1e3"` are `null` here and finite numbers there, while every string these
// accept `parseF64` accepts too. Choosing between them is therefore a choice of
// TYPE and never a choice of DIALECT: no string parses here and fails there.
// A caller with a scruffier input trims and rewrites first; std does not guess.
// (Same sentence as `parseF64`'s header, and the same reason.)
//
//   THE SUBSET IS ABOUT ACCEPTANCE, NOT ABOUT THE VALUE, and the difference is
//   this module's entire warrant. The two agree wherever the integer is exactly
//   representable as a double — which is NOT the same as "at or below 2^53":
//   measured, `"9007199254740994"` and `"18014398509481984"` (2^54) both agree,
//   because an even integer can be representable well past 2^53. They disagree
//   exactly where it is not: `parseI64("9223372036854775807")` is that integer
//   while `parseF64` of it is 2^63. And they disagree at `"-0"`, which is 0
//   here and −0.0 there — an integer type has no signed zero to preserve. A
//   caller picks the function whose TYPE it wants and gets that type's best
//   answer. Reading the subset rule as "the two are interchangeable" is exactly
//   the mistake the `parseF64` funnel was making before these existed.
//
//   "+" IS REFUSED, and this is the deliberate one — `Number("+3")` is 3, C's
//   `strtol` skips a leading "+", and Rust's `i64::from_str` accepts it, so the
//   refusal costs a spelling three mainstream readers allow. It is refused
//   anyway because `parseF64` refuses it (JSON's grammar has none, and RFC
//   8259's `int` production has none either), and every one of those three
//   readers is UNIFORM across its integer and float entry points. Accepting it
//   on only the integer side would make `std:fmt` the one number surface in
//   that list running two dialects, and would do it for the single character
//   where the two functions could disagree about a WELL-FORMED number rather
//   than about a type.
//
//   THE DIRECTION IS WHAT SETTLES IT, and it is this module's own argument for
//   the error channel, applied to the grammar: a std name has no deprecation
//   story, so a grammar can be WIDENED later and cannot be narrowed. Adding "+"
//   to both parsers on the day a consumer needs it breaks nothing; removing it
//   from one of them afterwards breaks every caller that spelled it. Refusing
//   now keeps both options; accepting now spends one of them on a case no
//   consumer in the tree has asked for. Note the consequence for the round
//   trip: `toString` never emits a "+", so nothing about
//   `parseI64(toString(n)) == n` depends on this choice either way.
//
//   LEADING ZEROS ARE ACCEPTED — `"007"` is 7 — matching `Number`, every
//   `strtol`, and `parseF64`'s own rule two sections down, and costing nothing.
//   This is the one place the grammar is wider than it strictly needs to be,
//   and it is inside `parseF64`'s grammar rather than beside it.
//
//   `"-0"` IS 0. There is no negative zero integer, so the sign is consumed and
//   nothing is left to record. (`parseF64` cannot say this: it answers −0.0,
//   which is a distinct f64 — the one FINITE value its own round trip excepts,
//   NaN being the other.)
//
//   EMPTY AND A LONE "-" ARE `null`, and so is a lone "+", which is simply not
//   a digit. `parseF64` answers `null` on all three.
//
// ── OUT OF RANGE IS `null`, AND THAT IS THE OPPOSITE OF `parseF64`'s RULE —
// which says out of range is NOT a parse failure and answers ±Infinity or ±0.
// The two rules disagree because the two TYPES do. A float has values that mean
// "larger than any of me": `"1e400"` → `Infinity` preserves what can be
// preserved, and `strtod`, `Number()` and Rust's `parse` all agree. An integer
// has no such value, so every candidate answer for `"9223372036854775808"` is a
// lie of a different kind:
//
//     wrap      → -9223372036854775808   a silent WRONG VALUE — the class this
//                                        repo files inventory rows for
//     saturate  →  9223372036854775807   also wrong, and indistinguishable from
//                                        the honest parse of the string one
//                                        smaller
//     null      →                        "this string denotes no i64", true
//
// Only the third is true, and it costs the caller nothing they were not already
// paying: the return is a union, so the null branch exists whether or not range
// rides on it. This does NOT put two meanings on one channel the way
// `parseF64`'s header warns against, because for an integer "out of range" and
// "not a number of this type" are the SAME statement — the type has no member
// that could denote the difference. For a float they are different statements,
// which is exactly why `parseF64` keeps them apart.
//
// ── `i64 | null` / `i32 | null` IS THE CHANNEL, for `parseF64`'s reason,
// unchanged: `error-handling-design.md` rules `T | null` for absence and
// `T | E` for a failure carrying information the caller needs. There is no such
// information here. A parse POSITION would be honest only if a prefix could
// succeed, and it cannot — these consume the whole string or fail — and after
// the paragraph above there is no second KIND to report either. Rust's
// `ParseIntError` does distinguish `Empty`/`InvalidDigit`/`PosOverflow`, and
// that is the alternative that was not taken: every consumer this is being
// built for branches on success, not on why. `i64 | ParseIntError` is a
// WIDENING of this signature that would cost existing callers one `is` branch
// — the direction std can still travel, unlike the reverse.
//
// ── TWO NAMES, NOT ONE, and neither is a narrow of the other. Three things
// forced it:
//
//   (1) THE RETURN TYPE IS THE POINT. A caller who wants an `i32` and is handed
//   an `i64 | null` has to narrow, and `v as i32` on this compiler is an
//   UNCHECKED wrapping truncation — measured 2026-09-01, `2147483648 as i32` is
//   `-2147483648` and `4294967296 as i32` is `0`. Handing every i32 caller that
//   cast is handing them the wrap this module just refused to perform.
//
//   (2) VL HAS NO RETURN-TYPE OVERLOADING AND NO RETURN-POSITION GENERIC. A
//   single `parseInt<T>(self: string): T | null` cannot be CALLED. Measured
//   2026-09-01, both arms: the DECLARATION alone compiles and runs (rc 0), and
//   adding `const v = parseInt("1")` is refused with `emitProgram:
//   monomorphize: a return type parameter of `parseInt` is not bound by any
//   parameter` — because nothing at the call site carries T. The refusal is at
//   the use, not the definition, which is why the declaration compiling is not
//   evidence against it. Type-directed reading is what serde stage 2's deriver
//   is for; a pair of names is what can be written today.
//
//   (3) IT MIRRORS THE RENDERER'S DOMAIN EXACTLY. `toString` renders `i32` and
//   `i64` (plus `boolean` and `f64`); the integer inverses cover the same two
//   widths and no more, and `parseF64` is the f64 one. That is the rubric's
//   "a width-suffixed family stays uniform", read across the two directions.
//
// `parseI32` is therefore NOT `parseI64` followed by a narrow: it range-checks
// in the WIDE type first and truncates only behind that check, where the
// truncation is provably lossless. The two share `parseBoundedInt`, so the
// module holds ONE grammar and ONE overflow argument rather than two that can
// drift apart.
//
// ── THE ARITHMETIC NEVER OVERFLOWS, by construction rather than by detection.
// The accumulator works in the NEGATIVE range — the same trick, and the same
// reason, as `renderI64` above: the negative half is the wider one, so i64 min
// is reachable as an intermediate while its positive counterpart never has to
// be. Each guard runs BEFORE the operation it protects, so nothing ever wraps.
// A wrapped SIGN is not used as a detector anywhere here and must not be: for a
// long enough digit string the accumulator wraps more than once and the sign
// comes back, so that test would pass on a value it should refuse.
//
//   AND THERE IS NO DIGIT CAP, unlike `parseF64`'s 800. That cap exists because
//   the float path holds the significand as a BIG INTEGER, so a megabyte of
//   digits is a megabyte of allocation — a denial-of-service surface for the
//   JSON reader both halves are being built for. This path's accumulator is one
//   fixed-width i64: a megabyte of digits is a megabyte of scanning, constant
//   memory, and a `null` from the range guard within the first twenty
//   iterations unless every one of them is a leading zero. Nothing to cap.
//
// ── WHAT IS NOT HERE. No radix but ten, matching `toString` — hex and binary
// reading is a different function family with its own prefix and case
// questions, and nothing in the tree asks for one. No unsigned parser: VL's
// integer surface is `i32`/`i64`, so a `u32` reader would be a name for a type
// this module cannot return. No `parseBoolean`: `"true"`/`"false"` is a
// two-string comparison a caller writes in one line, and a std name for it
// would have to rule on `"True"`, `"1"` and `"yes"` — questions the number
// grammar simply does not have. No prefix or partial parse (a `strtol`-style
// end position): a TOKENIZER wants that, `std:json` is the consumer that would
// name its shape, and it waits for that consumer rather than being guessed at
// now.

// i64's bounds. i64 min is spelled as max-minus-one because it has no decimal
// literal: `-9223372036854775808` lexes as a negation of a magnitude that
// overflows a positive i64 before the minus applies — the same fact `SIGN_BIT`
// records for f64 further down.
const I64_MAX: i64 = 9223372036854775807
const I64_MIN: i64 = -9223372036854775807 - 1
// i64 min divided by ten, truncated toward zero: the largest magnitude the
// accumulator may already hold and still survive a multiply by ten.
const I64_MIN_DIV10: i64 = -922337203685477580
// i32's bounds, held WIDE. They are compared against an i64 accumulator, and
// widening them here is exactly what lets `parseI32` reject before it truncates.
const I32_MAX_WIDE: i64 = 2147483647
const I32_MIN_WIDE: i64 = -2147483648

// The integer `self` spells, as an i64 — or `null` when `self` is not an
// integer in this section's grammar, or denotes one outside `lo`..=`hi`
// (inclusive). Both exported parsers ARE this function; they differ only in the
// bounds they pass and in the width they hand back.
//
// `lo <= 0 <= hi` IS A PRECONDITION, because each sign is checked against ONE
// bound: `lo` on the negative path, `hi` on the positive one. A range that does
// not contain zero is therefore NOT enforced — `parseBoundedInt("-3", -10, -5)`
// answers -3, not null. Both callers here straddle zero, so the check is exact
// for them; a third caller that does not (a bounded `u8`-valued field reader,
// say) has to add the other half. The symmetric version is not written because
// it needs `0 - lo`, which overflows at `lo == i64 min`, and no caller wants it.
function parseBoundedInt(self: string, lo: i64, hi: i64): i64 | null {
  const n = self.length
  if n == 0 { return null }
  let i = 0
  let neg = false
  // Only "-": a leading "+" is not part of this grammar (see the header — the
  // subset rule), and falls through to the digit loop, which refuses it.
  if self[0] == 45 {
    neg = true
    i = 1
  }
  // A sign with no digits after it denotes nothing.
  if i >= n { return null }

  // `acc` holds the value NEGATED — see the header: the negative half reaches
  // i64 min, so `"-9223372036854775808"` is an ordinary intermediate here while
  // the magnitude 9223372036854775808 never has to exist.
  let acc: i64 = 0
  while i < n {
    const c = self[i]
    // Spelled inline rather than through `isDigitByte` (which the f64 parser
    // uses further down) because this is the hot loop's first test and the
    // negated form lets the refusal return directly. One predicate, two
    // spellings — if a third appears, collapse them.
    if c < 48 || c > 57 { return null }
    const d: i64 = c - 48
    // Guard, THEN multiply: below this floor the product leaves i64 entirely.
    if acc < I64_MIN_DIV10 { return null }
    acc = acc * 10
    // Guard, THEN subtract. `I64_MIN + d` adds a value in 0..=9 to i64 min, so
    // the comparison's own arithmetic is always in range.
    if acc < I64_MIN + d { return null }
    acc = acc - d
    i = i + 1
  }

  if neg {
    if acc < lo { return null }
    return acc
  }
  // `0 - acc` is safe only after this test, because `acc` may be i64 min, whose
  // magnitude is not an i64. `hi` is at most i64 max, so `0 - hi` is always in
  // range and the whole comparison can stay in the negative half.
  if acc < 0 - hi { return null }
  0 - acc
}

// The i64 that the decimal integer `self` spells, or `null` when `self` is not
// one or does not fit. See the `TEXT → INTEGER` header for the grammar (leading
// zeros in; a leading `"+"`, whitespace, `"1.0"` and `"1e3"` out), for why out
// of range is `null` here while it is ±Infinity in `parseF64`, and for why the
// failure channel is `null` rather than an error struct.
//
// Exact for every accepted input by construction — no float exists at any step
// — which is the entire difference from routing integer text through
// `parseF64`: measured 2026-09-01, `parseF64("9223372036854775807")` is
// `9223372036854776000` while this answers `9223372036854775807`.
export function parseI64(self: string): i64 | null {
  parseBoundedInt(self, I64_MIN, I64_MAX)
}

// The i32 that the decimal integer `self` spells, or `null` when `self` is not
// one or does not fit in 32 bits. Same grammar as `parseI64`; `"2147483648"`
// and `"-2147483649"` are `null` rather than wrapped.
export function parseI32(self: string): i32 | null {
  const v = parseBoundedInt(self, I32_MIN_WIDE, I32_MAX_WIDE)
  if v is null { return null }
  // The only narrowing cast in this module, correct SOLELY because the bounds
  // above already refused everything outside i32. `as i32` is an unchecked
  // wrapping truncation on this compiler (header, measured), so moving this
  // line in front of the check would reintroduce the very wrap the null channel
  // exists to avoid.
  v as i32
}

// ── f64 ↔ TEXT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Serde stage 0 (`docs/serde-design.md` §Recommendation). Until this landed VL
// could not render or read a float at all: `"v=" + 0.1` was
// `operator '+' is not defined for string and f64`, and there was no parser
// anywhere in std. The shortest-round-trip printer that `print(0.1 + 0.2)` uses
// lives in the HOST's print sink, not in VL, so its result was never available
// as a VL string value. `docs/internals/std-design.md` D4 item 1 deferred this
// ("Ryu-class … explicitly DEFERRED — `print` keeps covering floats"); that
// deferral is now spent, and the paragraph there says so.
//
// ── THE STYLE IS ECMAScript `Number::toString`, radix 10, and that is a SPEC,
// not a preference. Two independent reasons pick it. (1) It is what the two
// hosts' print sinks already claim to implement — `scripts/vl-host/src/main.rs`
// names the clause and `tests/support/runWasm.ts` / `playground/src/runtime.ts`
// sink through JS `String(v)` literally — so `print(x.toString())` and `print(x)`
// are meant to be indistinguishable, and any other choice would make VL print
// a float two ways. (2) `String(v)` IS shortest-round-trip by specification, so
// the rule is pinned by a document rather than by an implementation.
//
// The rule, restated because the layout half is easy to get wrong: let `s` be
// the SHORTEST digit string (k digits, no radix point) and `n` the exponent
// with value = 0.s × 10^n. Then
//
//     k ≤ n ≤ 21   → the digits, then n−k zeros            ("100")
//     0 < n ≤ 21   → a radix point n digits in             ("1.5")
//     −6 < n ≤ 0   → "0." then −n zeros then the digits    ("0.000001")
//     otherwise    → exponential, exponent always signed   ("1e+21", "1e-7")
//
// and the specials: NaN → "NaN", ±∞ → "Infinity"/"-Infinity", and BOTH zeros →
// "0". Among equally short digit strings the spec takes the one closest to the
// value, and on an exact tie **the one whose last digit is even**.
//
// ── THE HOST'S `print` DISAGREES AT EXACT TIES, and it is the host that is
// wrong. Measured 2026-09-01 over 50,000 pseudo-random bit patterns: this
// module's rendering equals JS `String(v)` on 50,000 of 50,000, while the Rust
// host's `print` disagrees with JS on 14 of them — always an exact decimal tie,
// always rounding AWAY from even where the spec rounds to even. Smallest
// witness: the double with bits 4835952189745799117 is exactly
// 2023347301156851.25, so "…851.2" and "…851.3" are equidistant 17-digit
// candidates; the spec (and V8, and this module) print `2023347301156851.2`,
// the Rust host prints `2023347301156851.3`. The cause is that
// `js_number_to_string` re-formats digits it gets from Rust's `{:e}`, and
// Rust's shortest-formatter breaks that tie the other way; its own doc comment
// enumerates four Rust-vs-JS FORMATTING differences and does not know about
// this DIGIT one. Consequence: the same VL program prints differently under the
// two hosts for those values — a cross-host divergence that predates this
// module. This file follows the spec and the V8 host; the divergence is pinned
// by `tests/vl_std_float_text_test.ts` so it flips loudly when the host is
// fixed.
//
// ── ROUND TRIP: `parseF64(x.toString())` is bit-identical to `x` for every finite
// f64 EXCEPT −0, and returns a NaN for NaN. Both exceptions are the style's,
// not the algorithm's: the spec renders −0 as `"0"`, so the sign is gone before
// the parser sees it (JS `Number(String(-0))` is `+0` for the same reason), and
// NaN renders as `"NaN"` which carries no payload. Measured over the same
// 50,000 vectors: 50,000 of 50,000 round-trip under that rule, 0 exceptions.
//
// ── THE ALGORITHMS, and why these rather than Ryū.
//
// FORMATTING is Steele & White / Burger–Dybvig free-format printing over exact
// rational arithmetic: the value is held as R/S with the two half-ulp
// boundaries M+ and M−, all four exact big integers, and digits are generated
// until the emitted prefix already identifies the value uniquely within
// (v−M−, v+M+). Correctness is by construction rather than by table: no digit
// is emitted that the interval does not force, and the loop cannot stop early
// because stopping IS the interval test. Boundary inclusivity follows
// round-to-nearest-EVEN — an even significand owns its boundaries, an odd one
// does not — and the final digit takes the spec's closest-then-even rule.
//
// Ryū was the alternative and was NOT taken. Ryū is faster, but it needs ~1,240
// 64-bit table constants (two 128-bit power-of-five tables) and 64×64→128
// multiplication synthesised from 32-bit halves, and its correctness argument
// is "the table bounds were proven elsewhere". This one needs no table at all,
// no 128-bit multiply (limbs are 32 bits inside an i64, so every partial
// product fits with room), and its correctness argument is one paragraph of
// exact arithmetic that a reader can check against the code. A std module with
// no deprecation story should be the one that can be re-derived. The price is
// speed, and it is MEASURED rather than guessed — 100,000 iterations each, wall
// clock, minus the same program with the loop body removed:
//
//     rendering a random double        ~25 µs   (the 17-digit case; a short one
//                                                stops the digit loop sooner)
//     parsing on the fast path         < 1 µs   ("0.1")
//     parsing on the exact path        ~22 µs   ("2.2250738585072012e-308")
//
// Ryū would be roughly two orders faster at rendering. If a consumer ever names
// a throughput requirement it drops in behind this same signature; until one
// does, the table-free version is the one that can be checked by reading it.
//
// PARSING is Clinger's fast path over an exact fallback. When the significand
// has ≤ 15 digits and the decimal exponent is within ±22, both operands are
// exactly representable and one IEEE multiply or divide is one correctly
// rounded rounding — that is Clinger's theorem, and it covers `"0.1"` and most
// human-written numbers. Otherwise the value is held EXACTLY as num/den (big
// integers), the binary exponent is chosen from the bit lengths so the quotient
// lands in [2^52, 2^53), and a 55-step binary long division produces that
// quotient plus its exact remainder. Rounding compares 2·remainder against the
// divisor: greater rounds up, equal is a true tie broken to even, less rounds
// down. Nothing about that is approximate, so it is correct at every halfway
// case by construction.
//
// SIGNIFICANT DIGITS ARE CAPPED AT 800, with a `truncated` flag. That is the
// classic bound (767 digits suffice to separate any decimal from a double's
// rounding boundary; Go's `strconv` uses 800 for the same reason) plus slack:
// beyond it, dropped digits cannot move the comparison EXCEPT by turning an
// exact tie into a value strictly above it, which is exactly what the flag
// does. Without the cap a megabyte of digits would be a megabyte of big
// integer, which is a denial-of-service surface for the JSON reader this is
// being built for.
//
// ── THE GRAMMAR `parseF64` ACCEPTS is closed under `toString` — every string this
// module can PRODUCE parses back — and it is JSON's number grammar plus
// Infinity/NaN, NOT VL's literal grammar. Precisely:
//
//     "-"? ( "Infinity" | digits ("." digits)? ([eE] [+-]? digits)? )
//     "NaN"
//
// VL's literals are a strict superset in one direction: digit separators are
// legal VL (`1_000.5` and `1_0e1_0` both run — measured) and are REFUSED here,
// because a separator is a source-code affordance and this parser reads DATA.
// Deliberate exclusions: a leading "+", leading or trailing whitespace, a bare
// "1." or ".5" (VL's lexer refuses both — measured), "0x10" (a VL INTEGER
// literal, never a float one), digit separators, and "-NaN". A caller with a
// scruffier dialect trims and rewrites first; std does not guess.
// Leading zeros ARE accepted ("007", "0.5"), matching every strtod and costing
// nothing. If user-defined casts (error-handling-design.md §Future extensions
// (b), open) ever land, `s as f64` is a two-line delegation to THIS function,
// not a second parser — and the null channel is precisely what makes
// `s as? f64` read correctly. One float parser in the tree either way.
//
// ── `f64 | null` IS THE CHANNEL, and the reason it is not `f64 | ParseError`
// is that there is nothing to put in one. `error-handling-design.md` rules
// `T | null` for absence and `T | E` for "a failure with information the caller
// needs — an errno, a message, a parse position". A position would be honest
// only if a prefix could succeed, and it cannot: `parseF64` consumes the WHOLE
// string or fails, so the only fact is "this string denotes no f64", which the
// null already says. That is also where every mainstream float parser landed —
// Rust's `ParseFloatError` carries no position, Go's echoes the input, JS
// answers `NaN` — and the closest VL precedent is `Map[k]`, absence rather than
// diagnosis.
//
// `std:base64`, landing in the same slice, answers `u8[] | Base64Error`
// instead, and the two are not a drift: a base64 blob is long, machine-made
// and has four distinguishable ways to be wrong, so an offset and a kind
// locate a truncated transfer; a float literal is one token with one way to be
// wrong. (That module's header also records that `u8[] | null` does not lower
// on this compiler, which settled it there independently.)
//
// ── OUT OF RANGE IS NOT A PARSE FAILURE. Overflow answers ±Infinity and
// underflow answers ±0 — never null. `null` is the SYNTAX channel and nothing
// else, so `"1e400"` (a perfectly well-formed number that no f64 represents)
// answers `Infinity` exactly as `strtod`, `Number()` and Rust's `parse` all do,
// and a caller who wants overflow rejected tests the result. Reading a range
// error out of the same null that means "not a number" would have been a second
// meaning on one channel — the ambiguity `error-handling-design.md` exists to
// prevent.
//
// ── WHY `parseF64` LIVES HERE AND NOT IN A `std:num`, MEASURED. The header's
// missing-DCE paragraph applies again and the split does not pay. Same probe as
// that measurement — `import { toString } from "std:fmt"` plus one `n.toString()` on
// an i32, built to wasm:
//
//     before the f64 half               7,548 bytes
//     + the f64 FORMATTER              14,256   (+6,708 — forced: it IS `toString`)
//     + the PARSER                     17,062   (+2,806)
//
//     This ladder stops where the F64 half ended, which is why its last rung
//     is 17,062 and the module header's current figure is 17,645: the integer
//     parsers added 583 after it, and they are not part of the question this
//     ladder answers (whether `parseF64` should have gone to a `std:num`).
//     The endpoints are exact (probe below, re-run 2026-09-01); the two
//     sub-rungs were measured before a 16-byte fmt.vl edit, so their SPLIT is
//     indicative while their sum is pinned by the endpoints. The probe, run
//     from the repo root — note VL_STD, without which the host resolves std:
//     from the BINARY's own checkout and silently measures the wrong tree:
//       VL_STD=$PWD/std scripts/vl-host/target/release/vl build p.vl -o p.wasm
//       # optimized rungs (wasm-opt is NOT on PATH — CLAUDE.md):
//       VL_WASM_OPT=./node_modules/.bin/wasm-opt VL_STD=$PWD/std \
//         scripts/vl-host/target/release/vl build p.vl -O3 -o p.wasm
//       # p.vl:  import { toString } from "std:fmt"
//       #        const n = 42
//       #        print(n.toString())
//
// The formatter cannot move: a flat namespace has one `toString`, so the f64 arm
// and everything under it are in this module by construction. That makes the
// parser's own 2,822 bytes (at the `none` rung — at `-O3` the unused parser costs
// nothing measurable, which strengthens this answer) the entire question, and
// paying them buys the pair
// staying in one place — a reader who found `toString` finds its inverse beside it,
// and the exact-arithmetic helpers have one home rather than being exported from
// here to be reachable there. The real fix is whole-program dead-code
// elimination, which this header already names; a second module would buy 2.8 KB
// back by splitting a symmetric pair across two files forever.

// ── exact big naturals ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Little-endian limbs of 32 bits each, held in an `i64[]` so that a limb×small
// product has room: (2^32−1) × 10^9 is 4.29e18, inside i64's 9.22e18, which is
// why every multiplier below is capped at 10^9 and why no 128-bit multiply is
// needed anywhere in this file.
//
// HIGH ZERO LIMBS ARE LEFT IN PLACE rather than trimmed, because `.pop()` on an
// `i64[]` does not lower on this compiler. `i32[]` and `string[]` pop and run;
// an `i64[]` PARAMETER refuses loudly (`emitProgram: .pop receiver is not a
// list`) and an `i64[]` LOCAL is worse — `vl check` rc 0 and then invalid wasm,
// in three lines with nothing else in the program. Both arms are pinned by
// `scripts/capability-probes/i64-list-pop.vl`. `bnLen` is the consequence:
// every operation reads up to the highest non-zero limb rather than to
// `.length`. Delete it the day that probe runs; nothing else here depends on it.

const BN_MASK: i64 = 4294967295

// The number of limbs that carry value — the index of the highest non-zero
// limb, plus one. Zero is length 0.
function bnLen(a: i64[]): i32 {
  let i = a.length - 1
  while i >= 0 && a[i] == 0 { i = i - 1 }
  i + 1
}

function bnIsZero(a: i64[]): boolean { bnLen(a) == 0 }

// A fresh big natural holding `v`, which must not be negative.
function bnFromI64(v: i64): i64[] {
  const out: i64[] = []
  let x = v
  while x > 0 {
    out.push(x & BN_MASK)
    x = x >>> 32
  }
  out
}

function bnCopy(a: i64[]): i64[] {
  const out: i64[] = []
  let i = 0
  while i < a.length {
    out.push(a[i])
    i = i + 1
  }
  out
}

// -1, 0 or 1 as `a` is less than, equal to, or greater than `b`.
function bnCmp(a: i64[], b: i64[]): i32 {
  const la = bnLen(a)
  const lb = bnLen(b)
  if la != lb {
    if la < lb { return -1 }
    return 1
  }
  let i = la - 1
  while i >= 0 {
    if a[i] != b[i] {
      if a[i] < b[i] { return -1 }
      return 1
    }
    i = i - 1
  }
  0
}

// `a += b`, in place.
function bnAddInto(a: i64[], b: i64[]) {
  let carry: i64 = 0
  let i = 0
  while i < b.length || carry != 0 {
    if i >= a.length { a.push(0) }
    let s = a[i] + carry
    if i < b.length { s = s + b[i] }
    a[i] = s & BN_MASK
    carry = s >>> 32
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// `a -= b`, in place. The caller guarantees `a >= b`; a smaller `a` would
// borrow off the top and produce a garbage value rather than trapping, so
// every call site here is preceded by a `bnCmp`.
function bnSubInto(a: i64[], b: i64[]) {
  let borrow: i64 = 0
  let i = 0
  while i < a.length {
    let d = a[i] - borrow
    if i < b.length { d = d - b[i] }
    if d < 0 {
      d = d + 4294967296
      borrow = 1
    } else {
      borrow = 0
    }
    a[i] = d
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// `a *= m`, in place. `m` must be at most 10^9 — see the limb-width note above.
function bnMulSmall(a: i64[], m: i64) {
  let carry: i64 = 0
  let i = 0
  while i < a.length {
    const p = a[i] * m + carry
    a[i] = p & BN_MASK
    carry = p >>> 32
    i = i + 1
  }
  while carry != 0 {
    a.push(carry & BN_MASK)
    carry = carry >>> 32
  }
}

// 10^n for n in 0..=18, which is every n this file asks for.
function pow10i64(n: i32): i64 {
  let p: i64 = 1
  let i = 0
  while i < n {
    p = p * 10
    i = i + 1
  }
  p
}

// `a *= 10^k`, in place, in 10^9 chunks so the per-limb product stays in range.
function bnMulPow10(a: i64[], k: i32) {
  if bnIsZero(a) { return }
  let n = k
  while n >= 9 {
    bnMulSmall(a, 1000000000)
    n = n - 9
  }
  if n > 0 { bnMulSmall(a, pow10i64(n)) }
}

// `a <<= bits`, in place. The sub-limb shift runs first so the whole-limb move
// only ever relocates finished limbs.
function bnShlInto(a: i64[], bits: i32) {
  if bnIsZero(a) { return }
  const whole = bits / 32
  const rem = bits % 32
  if rem > 0 {
    let carry: i64 = 0
    let i = 0
    while i < a.length {
      const v = (a[i] << rem) | carry
      a[i] = v & BN_MASK
      carry = v >>> 32
      i = i + 1
    }
    if carry != 0 { a.push(carry) }
  }
  if whole > 0 {
    let j = 0
    while j < whole {
      a.push(0)
      j = j + 1
    }
    let i = a.length - 1
    while i >= whole {
      a[i] = a[i - whole]
      i = i - 1
    }
    let z = 0
    while z < whole {
      a[z] = 0
      z = z + 1
    }
  }
}

// `a >>= 1`, in place.
function bnShr1(a: i64[]) {
  let i = a.length - 1
  let carry: i64 = 0
  while i >= 0 {
    const v = a[i] | (carry << 32)
    a[i] = v >>> 1
    carry = v & 1
    i = i - 1
  }
}

function bnBitLen(a: i64[]): i32 {
  const n = bnLen(a)
  if n == 0 { return 0 }
  return (n - 1) * 32 + ((64 - clz(a[n - 1])) as i32)
}

// The big natural spelled by `d`, a list of decimal digit VALUES (0..=9).
// Nine digits at a time, so the multiplier stays inside `bnMulSmall`'s range.
function bnFromDigits(d: i32[]): i64[] {
  const out = bnFromI64(0)
  let i = 0
  while i < d.length {
    let chunk: i64 = 0
    let c = 0
    while c < 9 && i < d.length {
      const dv: i64 = d[i]
      chunk = chunk * 10 + dv
      i = i + 1
      c = c + 1
    }
    bnMulSmall(out, pow10i64(c))
    bnAddInto(out, bnFromI64(chunk))
  }
  out
}

// floor(a / b), leaving the exact remainder in `a`. Shift-and-subtract from
// the top, so the loop runs once per QUOTIENT bit rather than once per bit of
// `a`; the caller arranges a quotient of at most ~55 bits, which is what makes
// the i64 result and the loop bound safe.
function bnDivMod(a: i64[], b: i64[]): i64 {
  if bnCmp(a, b) < 0 { return 0 }
  const shift = bnBitLen(a) - bnBitLen(b)
  const d = bnCopy(b)
  bnShlInto(d, shift)
  let q: i64 = 0
  let i = shift
  while i >= 0 {
    q = q << 1
    if bnCmp(a, d) >= 0 {
      bnSubInto(a, d)
      q = q + 1
    }
    bnShr1(d)
    i = i - 1
  }
  q
}

// ── f64 → text ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The shortest digit string that identifies a value, with the exponent that
// places it: value = 0.d[0]d[1]… × 10^n.
type DecDigits = { d: i32[], n: i32 }

// `r + mp` against `s`, with the comparison inclusive when the significand is
// even (round-to-nearest-even hands an even significand its own boundaries).
function upperTest(r: i64[], mp: i64[], s: i64[], inclusive: boolean): boolean {
  const t = bnCopy(r)
  bnAddInto(t, mp)
  const c = bnCmp(t, s)
  if inclusive { return c >= 0 }
  c > 0
}

// Burger–Dybvig over exact rationals. `x` must be finite and strictly positive.
function shortestDigits(x: f64): DecDigits {
  const bits = f64bits(x)
  const be = ((bits >>> 52) & 2047) as i32
  const frac = bits & 4503599627370495
  let m = frac
  let e = -1074
  if be != 0 {
    m = frac + 4503599627370496
    e = be - 1075
  }
  const evenB = (m & 1) == 0
  // The gap BELOW is half the gap above exactly when the significand sits at
  // the bottom of a binade and there is a binade below it to fall into — so
  // `frac == 0` and an exponent above the subnormal one. Get this wrong and
  // powers of two render one digit too long.
  const smaller = frac == 0 && be > 1

  const r = bnFromI64(m)
  const s = bnFromI64(1)
  const mp = bnFromI64(1)
  const mm = bnFromI64(1)
  if e >= 0 {
    if smaller {
      bnShlInto(r, e + 2)
      bnShlInto(s, 2)
      bnShlInto(mp, e + 1)
      bnShlInto(mm, e)
    } else {
      bnShlInto(r, e + 1)
      bnShlInto(s, 1)
      bnShlInto(mp, e)
      bnShlInto(mm, e)
    }
  } else {
    if smaller {
      bnShlInto(r, 2)
      bnShlInto(s, 2 - e)
      bnShlInto(mp, 1)
    } else {
      bnShlInto(r, 1)
      bnShlInto(s, 1 - e)
    }
  }

  // `n` is the smallest integer with value + M+ ≤ 10^n. The float estimate is
  // deliberately biased LOW (log10(2) times the binary magnitude, minus one),
  // so the fixup below only ever climbs — which makes the result independent
  // of how tight the estimate is, and stops at the first n that qualifies, so
  // it is the minimal one.
  const bl = 64 - (clz(m) as i32)
  let k = ((floor(((bl + e - 1) as f64) * 0.3010299956639812)) as i32) - 1
  if k >= 0 {
    bnMulPow10(s, k)
  } else {
    bnMulPow10(r, 0 - k)
    bnMulPow10(mp, 0 - k)
    bnMulPow10(mm, 0 - k)
  }
  while upperTest(r, mp, s, evenB) {
    bnMulSmall(s, 10)
    k = k + 1
  }

  // One digit per turn: scale by ten, take the quotient by repeated
  // subtraction (it is 0..=9 by construction, so at most nine of them), then
  // ask whether the interval still needs another digit. `low` means the
  // remainder is already inside the lower boundary, `high` that adding one to
  // this digit lands inside the upper one; either alone terminates, and both
  // together take the closer of the two with the spec's even tie-break.
  //
  // The 25-digit bound is a floor, not a policy: a double never needs more
  // than 17, and a hypothetical 25th digit would still name the right value —
  // just not the shortest one.
  const digits: i32[] = []
  let done = false
  while !done && digits.length < 25 {
    bnMulSmall(r, 10)
    bnMulSmall(mp, 10)
    bnMulSmall(mm, 10)
    let d = 0
    while bnCmp(r, s) >= 0 {
      bnSubInto(r, s)
      d = d + 1
    }
    const cLow = bnCmp(r, mm)
    let low = cLow < 0
    if evenB { low = cLow <= 0 }
    const high = upperTest(r, mp, s, evenB)
    if low && high {
      const t = bnCopy(r)
      bnMulSmall(t, 2)
      const c = bnCmp(t, s)
      if c > 0 { d = d + 1 }
      else if c == 0 && (d % 2) != 0 { d = d + 1 }
      digits.push(d)
      done = true
    } else if low {
      digits.push(d)
      done = true
    } else if high {
      digits.push(d + 1)
      done = true
    } else {
      digits.push(d)
    }
  }

  // A terminating `d + 1` can carry out of 9. The scaling above is chosen so
  // this should not be reachable, and it is handled anyway because the failure
  // mode if it ever were would be a silently wrong digit rather than a crash.
  let ci = digits.length - 1
  let carrying = true
  while carrying && ci >= 0 {
    if digits[ci] != 10 {
      carrying = false
    } else {
      digits[ci] = 0
      if ci == 0 {
        digits[0] = 1
        k = k + 1
        carrying = false
      } else {
        digits[ci - 1] = digits[ci - 1] + 1
        ci = ci - 1
      }
    }
  }
  while digits.length > 1 && digits[digits.length - 1] == 0 { digits.pop() }

  { d: digits, n: k }
}

// The four-way layout of ECMA-262 `Number::toString` step 5, over a digit list
// and its exponent. See the rule table in this section's header.
function layoutDec(d: i32[], n: i32): string {
  const k = d.length
  const buf: i32[] = []
  if k <= n && n <= 21 {
    let i = 0
    while i < k {
      buf.push(48 + d[i])
      i = i + 1
    }
    let z = 0
    while z < n - k {
      buf.push(48)
      z = z + 1
    }
    return fromCodePoints(buf)
  }
  if n > 0 && n <= 21 {
    let i = 0
    while i < n {
      buf.push(48 + d[i])
      i = i + 1
    }
    buf.push(46)
    while i < k {
      buf.push(48 + d[i])
      i = i + 1
    }
    return fromCodePoints(buf)
  }
  if n > -6 && n <= 0 {
    buf.push(48)
    buf.push(46)
    let z = 0
    while z < 0 - n {
      buf.push(48)
      z = z + 1
    }
    let i = 0
    while i < k {
      buf.push(48 + d[i])
      i = i + 1
    }
    return fromCodePoints(buf)
  }
  buf.push(48 + d[0])
  if k > 1 {
    buf.push(46)
    let i = 1
    while i < k {
      buf.push(48 + d[i])
      i = i + 1
    }
  }
  const ex = n - 1
  if ex >= 0 {
    const up: i64 = ex
    return fromCodePoints(buf) + "e+" + renderI64(up)
  }
  const down: i64 = 0 - ex
  fromCodePoints(buf) + "e-" + renderI64(down)
}

// `toString`'s f64 arm. The sign is read off the BIT PATTERN rather than by
// comparing with zero, because `-0.0 < 0.0` is false and the exponent/fraction
// split is already in hand.
function renderF64(x: f64): string {
  const bits = f64bits(x)
  const be = ((bits >>> 52) & 2047) as i32
  const frac = bits & 4503599627370495
  if be == 2047 {
    if frac != 0 { return "NaN" }
    if bits < 0 { return "-Infinity" }
    return "Infinity"
  }
  // Both zeros render "0" — the spec's rule, and the reason -0 does not
  // survive a round trip through text.
  if be == 0 && frac == 0 { return "0" }
  const dd = shortestDigits(abs(x))
  const body = layoutDec(dd.d, dd.n)
  if bits < 0 { return "-" + body }
  body
}

// ── text → f64 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

const ONE64: i64 = 1
// 0x8000_0000_0000_0000. Spelled as a shift because i64 min has no decimal
// literal: its magnitude overflows a positive i64 before any minus applies.
const SIGN_BIT: i64 = ONE64 << 63
const BITS_INF: i64 = 9218868437227405312
const BITS_NAN: i64 = 9221120237041090560
const TWO53: i64 = 9007199254740992
const MAX_SIG_DIGITS = 800

function withSign(v: f64, neg: boolean): f64 {
  if neg { return f64fromBits(f64bits(v) | SIGN_BIT) }
  v
}

function isDigitByte(c: i32): boolean { c >= 48 && c <= 57 }

// 10^e, exactly, for e in 0..=22 — the range over which a power of ten is
// representable as an f64 (10^22 = 2^22 · 5^22 and 5^22 < 2^53). Each step is
// a product of two exactly representable values whose result is exactly
// representable, so IEEE rounding is a no-op. Callers must not exceed 22.
function pow10f(e: i32): f64 {
  let p = 1.0
  let i = 0
  while i < e {
    p = p * 10.0
    i = i + 1
  }
  p
}

// The f64 nearest to the number `self` spells, or `null` when `self` is not a
// number in this module's grammar. See the `f64 ↔ TEXT` header for the grammar,
// for why the failure channel is `null` rather than an error struct, and for
// why an out-of-range number answers ±Infinity or ±0 rather than failing.
//
// Correctly rounded for EVERY accepted input, halfway cases included: the fast
// path is exact-operands-single-rounding and the slow path is exact integer
// arithmetic with the tie broken to even. Measured 2026-09-01 against the
// ECMAScript oracle (`Number(s)`, itself specified as correctly rounded):
// **205,844 inputs, 0 disagreements** — 200,000 pseudo-random decimals across
// the whole exponent range and 5,844 adversarial ones, the adversarial set
// being exact midpoints between adjacent doubles, those midpoints ±1 ulp of
// decimal, midpoints with a 900-digit zero tail (the truncation path) and with
// a 1 past it, and the subnormal boundary. `tests/vl_std_float_text_test.ts`
// carries 3,391 of those plus 33 grammar rejections as the standing gate; the
// full campaign is a re-runnable measurement, not a merge gate.
export function parseF64(self: string): f64 | null {
  const n = self.length
  if n == 0 { return null }
  let i = 0
  let neg = false
  if self[0] == 45 {
    neg = true
    i = 1
  }
  if i >= n { return null }
  const c0 = self[i]
  if c0 == 73 {
    if self.slice(i, n) != "Infinity" { return null }
    return withSign(f64fromBits(BITS_INF), neg)
  }
  if c0 == 78 {
    // "NaN" only, unsigned: the renderer never produces a sign on it and VL
    // has no such literal, so "-NaN" would be a spelling nothing else uses.
    if neg { return null }
    if self != "NaN" { return null }
    return f64fromBits(BITS_NAN)
  }

  // `digs` collects SIGNIFICANT digits (leading zeros contribute only to the
  // exponent) and `exp10` is the power of ten the digit string is multiplied
  // by. Digits past the cap move `exp10` when they are integer places and set
  // `truncated` when they are non-zero — see the 800-digit note in the header.
  const digs: i32[] = []
  let exp10 = 0
  let truncated = false
  const istart = i
  while i < n && isDigitByte(self[i]) {
    const d = self[i] - 48
    // A LEADING zero of the integer part contributes nothing at all — not a
    // significant digit, and not a place either, so `"007"` is `7` with the
    // exponent untouched.
    if digs.length > 0 || d != 0 {
      if digs.length < MAX_SIG_DIGITS {
        digs.push(d)
      } else {
        // Past the cap an integer digit is still a PLACE, so it moves the
        // exponent; only a non-zero one moves the value.
        exp10 = exp10 + 1
        if d != 0 { truncated = true }
      }
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  if i == istart { return null }
  if i < n && self[i] == 46 {
    i = i + 1
    const fstart = i
    while i < n && isDigitByte(self[i]) {
      const d = self[i] - 48
      // Every fraction digit is a place, INCLUDING the leading zeros of
      // `"0.0012"` — they move the exponent without becoming significant.
      // Past the cap the exponent must NOT move (the place is already accounted
      // for by the digits that were kept), so only truncation is recorded.
      if digs.length >= MAX_SIG_DIGITS {
        if d != 0 { truncated = true }
      } else {
        if digs.length > 0 || d != 0 { digs.push(d) }
        exp10 = exp10 - 1
      }
      i = i + 1
    }
    if i == fstart { return null }
  }
  if i < n && (self[i] == 101 || self[i] == 69) {
    i = i + 1
    let es = 1
    if i < n && self[i] == 43 {
      i = i + 1
    } else if i < n && self[i] == 45 {
      es = -1
      i = i + 1
    }
    const estart = i
    let ev = 0
    while i < n && isDigitByte(self[i]) {
      // Saturating: any exponent past a million is already decided by the
      // range gate below, and letting it run would overflow the i32.
      if ev < 1000000 { ev = ev * 10 + (self[i] - 48) }
      i = i + 1
    }
    if i == estart { return null }
    exp10 = exp10 + es * ev
  }
  // Trailing anything — a unit, a stray digit separator, whitespace — is a
  // rejection rather than a partial parse.
  if i != n { return null }

  if digs.length == 0 { return withSign(0.0, neg) }
  // The magnitude gate, before any big integer exists: the value lies in
  // [10^(dmag-1), 10^dmag), the largest f64 is under 10^309 and anything below
  // 10^-350 is far under half the smallest subnormal.
  const dmag = exp10 + digs.length
  if dmag > 310 { return withSign(f64fromBits(BITS_INF), neg) }
  if dmag < -350 { return withSign(0.0, neg) }

  // Clinger's fast path: both operands exact, one rounding.
  if digs.length <= 15 && exp10 >= -22 && exp10 <= 22 {
    let acc: i64 = 0
    let j = 0
    while j < digs.length {
      const dv: i64 = digs[j]
      acc = acc * 10 + dv
      j = j + 1
    }
    let v = acc as f64
    if exp10 > 0 { v = v * pow10f(exp10) }
    if exp10 < 0 { v = v / pow10f(0 - exp10) }
    return withSign(v, neg)
  }

  // The exact path. value = num / den, both integers, no rounding yet.
  const num = bnFromDigits(digs)
  const den = bnFromI64(1)
  if exp10 > 0 { bnMulPow10(num, exp10) }
  if exp10 < 0 { bnMulPow10(den, 0 - exp10) }

  // Choose the binary exponent so the quotient has 53 bits. The bit-length
  // difference brackets log2(num/den) within one, so the quotient lands in
  // [2^52, 2^54) and at most a couple of corrections are needed; -1074 is the
  // floor because no double has a smaller exponent, and a quotient under 2^52
  // there is exactly what a subnormal is.
  let e2 = bnBitLen(num) - bnBitLen(den) - 53
  if e2 < -1074 { e2 = -1074 }
  let q: i64 = 0
  let rem = bnFromI64(0)
  let dn = bnFromI64(0)
  let settled = false
  while !settled {
    if e2 + 1075 >= 2047 { return withSign(f64fromBits(BITS_INF), neg) }
    rem = bnCopy(num)
    dn = bnCopy(den)
    if e2 > 0 { bnShlInto(dn, e2) }
    if e2 < 0 { bnShlInto(rem, 0 - e2) }
    q = bnDivMod(rem, dn)
    if q >= TWO53 { e2 = e2 + 1 } else { settled = true }
  }

  // Round to nearest, ties to even — except that a truncated tail means the
  // true value is strictly above the tie, so it rounds up.
  const t = bnCopy(rem)
  bnMulSmall(t, 2)
  const c = bnCmp(t, dn)
  let up = false
  if c > 0 {
    up = true
  } else if c == 0 {
    if truncated { up = true }
    else if (q & 1) != 0 { up = true }
  }
  if up { q = q + 1 }
  if q >= TWO53 {
    q = q / 2
    e2 = e2 + 1
  }
  if e2 + 1075 >= 2047 { return withSign(f64fromBits(BITS_INF), neg) }
  // One expression for both classes: with q in [2^52, 2^53) the carry out of
  // the significand IS the exponent field's low bit, and with e2 pinned at
  // -1074 the term vanishes and q lands in the fraction field, which is the
  // subnormal encoding.
  const be2: i64 = e2 + 1074
  return withSign(f64fromBits((be2 << 52) + q), neg)
}
